% Sucker Act
%
% The Logical English twin of a Blawx encoding, written by the Blawx translator.
% Each rule is a rule of the Act as Blawx encodes it,
% in the words of Blawx's own sentence forms, citing its section; the project's
% tests are the scenarios, with Blawx's own answers as the expectations.
%
% Source: the Blawx project "Numerical Constraints" (numerical_constraints.yaml, 4 sections), an example of
%         Blawx v1.6.22-alpha (Lexpedite, MIT licence), commit 3de892f67854292b304c9a55c5e2cd2058d3d418.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a person* may vote at *a number* years of age.
    *a person* is a person; scenario element.
    *a person* is *a number* years of age; scenario element.
    *a person* must pay taxes at *a number* years of age.
    *a person* is a sucker.

the knowledge base numerical_constraints includes:

the text of the Sucker Act is at "sources/numerical_constraints.md".

the text of the Blawx project is at "sources/numerical_constraints.yaml".

% The Act, section by section.
rule section_1 with provenance the Sucker Act at section 1:
a person may vote at a number N years of age if
    the person is a person
    and the person is N years of age
    and N >= 18.

rule section_2 with provenance the Sucker Act at section 2:
a person must pay taxes at a number N years of age if
    the person is a person
    and the person is N years of age
    and N >= 16.

rule section_3 with provenance the Sucker Act at section 3:
a person is a sucker if
    the person is a person
    and the person must pay taxes at a number years of age
    and it is not the case that the person may vote at the number years of age.

scenario sucker is, as stated in the Blawx project at test sucker:
    % pending — abductive: the test lets s(CASP) assume person/1, age/2 (#abducible); Blawx's answers are hypotheses, which an LE scenario states as facts or not at all:
    % sucker expects answers ["a person is a sucker"].

